  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 328 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nEarly next week, a deep cutoff low swings through the southwest, \npushing the crest of the weak ridge through the Ozarks. This will \nallow for highs to warm a few degrees for Monday, into the low 80s \nfor most of the area. By Tuesday, we lie in a southeast flow regime, \ndirectly in the path of shortwave energy that is shed from the \napproaching low. Moisture is pulled into the Plains by this low, \naided by a dying Hurricane Polo that begins to Fujiwara with it\nand is eventually absorbed into the circulation. This dynamic \nis set to bring a substantial rain event to the southwestern \nportion of the country. For us, it manifests as a classic \nmidlatitude system that will hopefully have the chops to bring \nsome relief to the drought- stricken areas of the Ozarks.\n\nShortwave energy forces scattered precipitation as early at\nTuesday primarily north of I-44, but the passage of the warm \nfront overnight places us securely in the warm sector through \nthe day Wednesday, bringing widespread PoPs 40-70% (for now). \nThe system looks to stall over Missouri for a while, keeping \nPoPs above 50% and as high as 80% through Friday morning. The \nfront does not appear to be in a hurry to leave. Anywhere from 2\ninches (south central Missouri) to 4 inches (west central \nMissouri) of rain are expected from Tuesday to Friday. For some \ncontext, the NBM 72-hour QPF probabilities (Tuesday 7am through \nFriday 7am) indicate a 50-90% chance of at least 1 inch, 30-80% \nchance of at least 2 inches, and 15-60% chance of at least 3 \ninches (lower probabilities south central Missouri, higher \nprobabilities west central Missouri). Portions of the Ozarks \nhave been included in the Weather Prediction Center's Excessive \nRainfall Outlook for Wednesday.",
